<!--
Behavior Modification Assignment, Handout 1 of 3.

Screen-per-question, following the paper handout's numbering (1, 2, 3, 4a, 4b,
4c, 5, 6). Two things differ deliberately from the paper version:

  * The paper handout is worth 50 points, 5 of which are for uploading photos
    of the handwritten pages alongside a typed copy. That requirement is an
    artifact of a paper workflow and has no analogue here, so the scored items
    below total the remaining 45.

  * Question 6 asks students to reuse the antecedents from 4a and the
    consequences from 4c. On paper this is the most commonly lost item —
    students introduce new ones instead of reusing their own. Here their own
    answers are shown on the screen via <Ref>, so the requirement is visible
    rather than remembered.

EVERY <LLMAction> PROMPT BODY IN THIS FILE IS GENERATED. Do not hand-edit one:
the next `--write` will overwrite it, and `--check` fails in the meantime.

  ~/code/molly_scoring/scorer/olx_prompts.py --check   # are they current?
  ~/code/molly_scoring/scorer/olx_prompts.py --write   # regenerate

The source is `rubric_hN.py` in that directory, which scores the paper
submissions item-by-item and measures itself against 20 rows of human grading
per handout. Each prompt follows `score.py:build_prompt`'s skeleton — item
header, question as asked, credit components, deduction code table, every
guidance bullet, worked examples, cross-item context — copied verbatim, so the
two implementations run the SAME rubric and a comparison between them means
something. To change what a prompt says, change the rubric. Every difference
that remains is listed, with its reason, in scorer/EQUIVALENCE.md.

That verbatim material is what carries the calibration: criteria the scoring
dictionary never states but the graders applied anyway (a Q1 reason must be a
negative consequence, not an aspiration; Q2's reasons must be benefits; Q3's
Measurable needs a tracking method; Q4b is judged on framing rather than
category; Q5 credits form over insight), the graders' own decided cases quoted
as written, and Q6's three worked examples. THREE is the measured optimum on
Q6 — a fourth example made agreement worse (62% -> 44%), and five were worse
still. Do not add more without re-measuring.

The canonical feedback wording in each prompt is the scoring dictionary's own
phrasing, so a student who fixes what the LLM flagged meets the same words if
the same gap is graded later. The prompt carries the point values because the
rubric does; it forbids the model from repeating them to the student.

Each item declares its checks as a `slots` sheet on the LLMAction, which makes
them required properties of a strict JSON schema rather than a request in
prose. That is the same fix that worked in the scorer, for the same reason: the
failure mode is not misjudging a check, it is answering two of eight and
writing fluent feedback about the two. The student sees the model's prose
followed by the verdicts, so which parts were found is visible rather than
inferred. A SlotSheetGrader beside each action turns that sheet into a score.
See LLMAction.md for the attribute format.
-->

<Sequential id="bmod_handout1" title="BMod Handout 1: Defining Behaviors, ABCs, and SMART Goals" launchable="true">

  <!-- ============= Screen: Choose an unwanted target behavior ============= -->

  <Vertical id="bmod_h1_choose" title="Choose your behavior">
    <Markdown id="bmod_h1_intro">
# Behavior Modification Assignment: Handout 1

Over {{corpus:Q3/p12:timebound:40:59:sha=e7390a67f3cf}} you will run a behavior modification intervention on
your **own** behavior: one week of baseline data collection, then three weeks
of intervention.

Two ground rules:

* You will intervene on your own behavior, not anyone else's.
* Do not choose an intervention that could cause you harm.

Start by choosing the unwanted target behavior (UTB) you want to change.
    </Markdown>

    <CapaProblem id="bmod_h1_utb_problem" title="Your unwanted target behavior" showanswer="never">
      <CorrectGrader id="bmod_h1_utb_grader">
        <Markdown id="bmod_h1_utb_prompt">Which behavior will you work on?</Markdown>
        <ChoiceInput id="bmod_h1_utb">
          <Key id="bmod_h1_utb_sleep" value="lack of sleep">Lack of sleep</Key>
          <Key id="bmod_h1_utb_exercise" value="lack of exercise">Lack of exercise</Key>
        <Key id="bmod_h1_utb_produce" value="{{corpus:Q1/p12:response:31:69:sha=67aefa440274}} vegetables">Insufficient consumption of fruits and vegetables</Key>
        <Key id="bmod_h1_utb_screens" value="spending too much time on electronic devices">Spending too much time on electronic devices</Key>
        </ChoiceInput>
      </CorrectGrader>
    </CapaProblem>
  </Vertical>

  <!-- ============= Screen: Question 1 - define the UTB ===================== -->

  <Vertical id="bmod_h1_q1" title="1. Define your unwanted target behavior">
    <Markdown id="bmod_h1_q1_prompt">
## 1. Define your unwanted target behavior and explain WHY you chose it

Write **one sentence** describing your unwanted target behavior, then **at
least three sentences** explaining why you chose to change it.

Your reasons should be things that go wrong because of the behavior — symptoms
you experience when you engage in it, or health issues that can arise from
continuing it.
    </Markdown>

    <TextArea id="bmod_h1_q1_response" rows="7"
              placeholder="{{corpus:Q1/p3:response:0:30:sha=c5fd6758ccc7}}..." />

    <LLMFeedback id="bmod_h1_q1_feedback" />
    <ActionButton id="bmod_h1_q1_btn" label="Check my answer">
      <LLMAction id="bmod_h1_q1_llm" target="bmod_h1_q1_feedback"
                 slots="utb_stated:Your unwanted target behavior stated explicitly@2|harms_listed:How many statements name a negative effect of the behavior:3/2/1/0|benefits_listed:How many name a benefit you expect from changing:3/2/1/0|reasons_given:How many separate reasons you give:3/2/1/0|reason_1:First reason — a negative effect of the behavior@1|reason_2:Second reason@1|reason_3:Third reason@1|uncertain:Any judgement you were unsure about:no/yes"
                 counts="reasons_given:reason_1,reason_2,reason_3">
You are an experienced teaching assistant grading Handout 1 of the Behavior Modification Assignment: defining behaviours, the ABCs of a functional behavioural analysis, and SMART goals.
This is PSYC 1030 (General Psychology, intro level, first-year students).

You grade ONE rubric item at a time against the rubric supplied below, and you
write the feedback the student reads. Return the JSON object the schema
requires: the `checks` sheet first, then `feedback`.

Rules you must follow:
1. Judge the item check by check. For every check on the sheet below, decide
   its verdict and quote the span of the response that settles it in that
   check's `evidence`. Quote verbatim; never paraphrase into the evidence
   field. For an unsatisfied check, say briefly what you looked for.
2. The deduction table below is the course's canonical wording for each way
   this item goes wrong. It is not a ledger for you to fill in — name any gap
   in `feedback` using that table's own words, so a student who fixes what you
   flagged meets the same phrasing if the same gap is graded later. The CODE
   beside each entry is an internal label: use the wording, and never put the
   code or its point value in front of the student.
3. Do NOT output a score. The score is computed from your checks.
4. `feedback` must be consistent with `checks`: do not praise something you
   marked unsatisfied, and do not fault something you marked satisfied.
5. Grade what is written, generously but not charitably: these are first-year
   students, so clumsy phrasing that clearly conveys the required idea earns
   credit, but a required element that is absent is absent.
6. If the response is empty, mark every check unsatisfied and say so plainly.
7. If the student describes something that could harm them (skipping meals,
   punishing themselves by withholding food or sleep, etc.), say so warmly in
   `feedback` and suggest a safer version. That is a safety note, not a fault
   in their work, and it never changes a verdict.
8. Where the grading guidance below tells you to set `escalate` or to write an
   `advisory_note`, there is no such field here: put the remark in `feedback`
   instead, and where the sheet carries an `uncertain` check, set it to `yes`.

Write `feedback` to the student, in the second person, warm and specific. Say
which requirements are met, name any that are missing, and quote their own
words when you point something out. It must read as prose written to them: no
deduction codes, no point values, no score, and no check keys.

# Rubric item Q1 — 5 points

## Question asked of the student
Define your unwanted target behavior (UTB) and explain WHY you chose this particular UTB for intervention. Requires 1 sentence describing the UTB and at least 3 sentences explaining why you chose to change this behavior.

## Credit components
- `utb_stated` (2 pt)
- `harms_listed`
- `benefits_listed`
- `reasons_given`
- `reason_1` (1 pt)
- `reason_2` (1 pt)
- `reason_3` (1 pt)

## Deduction codes — the course's canonical wording for each gap
- `UTB_NOT_ON_LIST` (-5): You did not choose an UTB from the provided list (lack of sleep, lack of exercise, {{corpus:Q1/p12:response:31:69:sha=67aefa440274}} vegetables, spending too much time on electronics). Please choose one of these behaviors for you to modify.
- `UTB_NOT_STATED` (-2): What is your UTB? This needs to be explicitly stated.
- `REASON_MISSING` (-1) [repeatable]: You did not provide 3 separate reasons as to why you chose to change this UTB. These should be negative consequences of engaging in the UTB.

## Grading guidance
- The UTB must be one of the four from the closed list.

## The checklist to return (`checks`)
Return a verdict for EVERY one of these, in this order, BEFORE you write
`feedback` — a response can fail most of them and still read fluently. Each
check's verdicts are listed with the satisfied one first, unless a note above
says the check reports an identity rather than a judgement.

- `utb_stated` — `met`/`absent`/`unclear`: {{corpus:Q1/p10:response:0:31:sha=f748d9d9ddd6}} identified as the student's own target, not merely mentioned as the cause of some effect. Judge ONLY the response text the student wrote. Where the UTB also appears in this prompt as a separate field or context line, that does NOT satisfy this check — the question is whether the RESPONSE names the behaviour as its target. A response that opens straight into effects ("{{corpus:Q1/p20:response:0:36:sha=7f8a552a724d}} tired...") fails; a sentence of the form "{{corpus:Q1/p3:response:0:30:sha=c5fd6758ccc7}} X" satisfies it
- `harms_listed` — `3`/`2`/`1`/`0`: HOW MANY statements name a NEGATIVE EFFECT of the unwanted behaviour — something that goes wrong because of it, or a symptom of it. "{{corpus:Q1/p1:response:280:337:sha=1946589e2f0b}} class" is one. A reason is a whole STATEMENT: a clause that merely continues one is part of it, so "{{corpus:Q1/p5:response:161:243:sha=bfd41c6d673e:shape=C7ef800000000000000}} weight" is ONE, while two effects merely joined by "and" are two. Do NOT count a restatement that the student struggles with the behaviour (participant 1) or a behaviour performed DURING it (participant 2). Answer 3 for three or more
- `benefits_listed` — `3`/`2`/`1`/`0`: HOW MANY statements name a BENEFIT the student expects from changing. The test is GETS versus DOES: a benefit is something the student GETS — "{{corpus:Q1/p10:response:155:189:sha=cedcf4a87d7a}} myself", "{{corpus:Q1/p16:response:256:285:sha=30187edf82d0}} person". A statement of what they intend to DO names the goal behaviour itself and is NOT a benefit of it, however much it sounds like a wish. Do NOT count a restatement of the goal ("{{corpus:Q1/p10:response:117:146:sha=be46148ce74e}} active"), the struggle ("something I have been struggling with"), or background about how the behaviour came about ("I used to exercise {{corpus:Q1/p16:response:90:102:sha=0264516373dc}} sports"). Answer 3 for three or more
- `reasons_given` — `3`/`2`/`1`/`0`: HOW MANY reasons count. The rule is CONDITIONAL on the two counts above. If `harms_listed` is 1 or more the answer IS `harms_listed`, and benefits do not add to it — a response with one harm and two benefits counts 1, which is what participant 6 scored. Only when `harms_listed` is 0 does the answer become `benefits_listed` instead, which is how participants 9, 10 and 16 were credited. Never answer 0 when the student offered anything of either kind. Answer 3 for three or more
- `uncertain` — `no`/`yes`: `yes` if any judgement above was a close call — this is rule 8's channel

DO NOT ANSWER `reason_1`, `reason_2`, `reason_3` individually. Answer `reasons_given` — HOW MANY you found — and the grader awards that many of them. Count them the way the guidance above says to, in one judgement over the whole response, rather than deciding each in isolation.

## The behavior they chose from the list
Chosen from the four on the list, before question 1.
<Ref id="bmod_h1_q1_ref_utb" target="bmod_h1_utb" />

## Student response to grade (item Q1)
<Ref id="bmod_h1_q1_ref" target="bmod_h1_q1_response" />
      </LLMAction>
      <!-- Scores the sheet this action publishes; costs and the item
           total live on the sheet, this block only does the arithmetic. -->
      <SlotSheetGrader id="bmod_h1_q1_grader" target="bmod_h1_q1_feedback" />
    </ActionButton>
  </Vertical>

  <!-- ============= Screen: Question 2 - define the WGB ===================== -->

  <Vertical id="bmod_h1_q2" title="2. Define your wanted goal behavior">
    <Markdown id="bmod_h1_q2_prompt">
## 2. Define your wanted goal behavior

Your wanted goal behavior (WGB) is the behavior you hope to *strengthen*. It
should be the opposite of your unwanted target behavior — if your UTB is not
drinking enough water, your WGB is drinking more water.

Write **one sentence** describing your wanted goal behavior, then **at least
three sentences** explaining why you chose to strengthen it. These reasons
should be the *benefits* of the goal behavior, not the harms of the unwanted
one.
    </Markdown>

    <Markdown id="bmod_h1_q2_recall">
Your unwanted target behavior was: **{{@bmod_h1_utb.value}}**
    </Markdown>

    <TextArea id="bmod_h1_q2_response" rows="7"
              placeholder="My wanted goal behavior is..." />

    <LLMFeedback id="bmod_h1_q2_feedback" />
    <ActionButton id="bmod_h1_q2_btn" label="Check my answer">
      <LLMAction id="bmod_h1_q2_llm" target="bmod_h1_q2_feedback"
                 slots="!wgb_is_counterpart:Goal behavior is the opposite of your unwanted behavior|wgb_inverts_utb:Goal behavior states the opposite of your unwanted behavior@2|reasons_listed:How many statements you offer as reasons:3/2/1/0|reasons_failing:How many of those are not a benefit of the goal:0/1/2/3|reasons_given:How many separate benefits you give:3/2/1/0|reason_1:First benefit of the goal behavior@1|reason_2:Second benefit@1|reason_3:Third benefit@1|uncertain:Any judgement you were unsure about:no/yes"
                 counts="reasons_given:reason_1,reason_2,reason_3">
You are an experienced teaching assistant grading Handout 1 of the Behavior Modification Assignment: defining behaviours, the ABCs of a functional behavioural analysis, and SMART goals.
This is PSYC 1030 (General Psychology, intro level, first-year students).

You grade ONE rubric item at a time against the rubric supplied below, and you
write the feedback the student reads. Return the JSON object the schema
requires: the `checks` sheet first, then `feedback`.

Rules you must follow:
1. Judge the item check by check. For every check on the sheet below, decide
   its verdict and quote the span of the response that settles it in that
   check's `evidence`. Quote verbatim; never paraphrase into the evidence
   field. For an unsatisfied check, say briefly what you looked for.
2. The deduction table below is the course's canonical wording for each way
   this item goes wrong. It is not a ledger for you to fill in — name any gap
   in `feedback` using that table's own words, so a student who fixes what you
   flagged meets the same phrasing if the same gap is graded later. The CODE
   beside each entry is an internal label: use the wording, and never put the
   code or its point value in front of the student.
3. Do NOT output a score. The score is computed from your checks.
4. `feedback` must be consistent with `checks`: do not praise something you
   marked unsatisfied, and do not fault something you marked satisfied.
5. Grade what is written, generously but not charitably: these are first-year
   students, so clumsy phrasing that clearly conveys the required idea earns
   credit, but a required element that is absent is absent.
6. If the response is empty, mark every check unsatisfied and say so plainly.
7. If the student describes something that could harm them (skipping meals,
   punishing themselves by withholding food or sleep, etc.), say so warmly in
   `feedback` and suggest a safer version. That is a safety note, not a fault
   in their work, and it never changes a verdict.
8. Where the grading guidance below tells you to set `escalate` or to write an
   `advisory_note`, there is no such field here: put the remark in `feedback`
   instead, and where the sheet carries an `uncertain` check, set it to `yes`.

Write `feedback` to the student, in the second person, warm and specific. Say
which requirements are met, name any that are missing, and quote their own
words when you point something out. It must read as prose written to them: no
deduction codes, no point values, no score, and no check keys.

# Rubric item Q2 — 5 points

## Question asked of the student
Define your wanted goal behavior (WGB) that you hope to strengthen. Requires 1 sentence describing the WGB and at least 3 sentences explaining why you chose to strengthen it.

## Credit components
- `wgb_is_counterpart` **GATE**: The goal behaviour concerns the SAME behaviour as the Q1 UTB. Fail this ONLY when it is a different behaviour altogether — "connect more with people and restart reading books" against a UTB of screen time. A goal in the right territory that simply does not invert the behaviour still passes this: that is WGB_NOT_OPPOSITE on `wgb_inverts_utb`, worth 2, not the whole item. When this DOES fail the goal is unstated and its reasons cannot count either, so WGB_UNRELATED stands INSTEAD of WGB_NOT_OPPOSITE plus reason deductions, never alongside them — it cost participant 7 the whole item
- `wgb_inverts_utb` (2 pt): The WGB is explicitly stated AND inverts the behaviour the UTB names — not merely a goal in the same territory, and not an outcome of the behaviour. Judge the BEHAVIOUR it concerns against the behaviour the UTB names: a goal that names a different activity fails (reading and socialising against a UTB of screen time), and so does one that names a different measure (losing twenty pounds against a UTB of lack of exercise). A goal stating the same behaviour's positive state PASSES — "get back in shape" against a UTB of {{corpus:Q3/p10:action:42:61:sha=00b9bd1557d3}} the inversion, phrased as a state. Full credit is the same behaviour turned around: UTB lack of sleep -> "get eight hours of sleep"; UTB lack of exercise -> "{{corpus:Q2/p6:response:30:57:sha=c00c372b3981}} week". "{{corpus:Q2/p10:response:31:56:sha=2bd894e2eb1a:shape=C1}}" against lack of exercise cost participant 10 two points — it never says exercise more
- `reasons_listed`: HOW MANY statements the response offers as reasons, before judging any of them. Count what is on the page
- `reasons_failing`: Of those, HOW MANY are not a benefit of the goal behaviour — because they restate the UTB's harm, or repeat another one
- `reasons_given`: HOW MANY of the listed statements are a real benefit of the GOAL behaviour: `reasons_listed` minus `reasons_failing`. A statement that restates the harm of the unwanted behaviour is not a benefit of the goal — "not exercising makes me feel lazy" is a reason to drop the UTB, not a benefit of exercising. Whether one sentence holds one benefit or two is STRUCTURAL: a second half that is a knock-on effect of the first is ONE ("{{corpus:Q2/p19:response:290:311:sha=4a878de5257c}} in {{corpus:Q2/p19:response:319:347:sha=c5e6c468e8bc:shape=C1ef80}} learning"), while two independent benefits merely joined by "and" are TWO ("{{corpus:Q2/p6:response:147:205:sha=ff93405cc948:shape=C380000000000}} also"). A restatement of the GOAL is not a benefit of it either — "{{corpus:Q2/p6:response:275:320:sha=447eb5403401}}" against a goal of being more active cost participant 6 a point. Statements about why the UTB is bad belong to Q1 and earn nothing here; participant 3 lost all three that way. Answer 3 for three or more
- `reason_1` (1 pt): First reason — a benefit of the goal behaviour
- `reason_2` (1 pt): Second reason
- `reason_3` (1 pt): Third reason

## Deduction codes — the course's canonical wording for each gap
- `WGB_UNRELATED` (-5): Your WGB should be the opposite of your UTB. You also need three reasons why you chose the WGB.
- `WGB_NOT_OPPOSITE` (-2): What is your WGB? This needs to be explicitly stated. Your WGB should be the opposite of your UTB (e.g., UTB = not drinking enough water; WGB = drinking more water).
- `REASON_MISSING` (-1) [repeatable]: You did not provide 3 separate reasons as to why you chose to strengthen this WGB. These should be the benefits of engaging in the WGB.

## The checklist to return (`checks`)
Return a verdict for EVERY one of these, in this order, BEFORE you write
`feedback` — a response can fail most of them and still read fluently. Each
check's verdicts are listed with the satisfied one first, unless a note above
says the check reports an identity rather than a judgement.

- `wgb_is_counterpart` **GATE** — `met`/`absent`/`unclear`: the WGB_UNRELATED test, and only that: is the goal behavior about a DIFFERENT behavior altogether from the unwanted one? A goal in the right territory that simply fails to invert the behavior still satisfies this — that is WGB_NOT_OPPOSITE on `wgb_inverts_utb`, worth 2, not the whole item. Not satisfied means the whole item is that finding
- `wgb_inverts_utb` — `met`/`absent`/`unclear`: TWO ways to fail, and the second is the common one. (i) no goal behavior is stated at all. (ii) a goal IS stated but does not invert the behaviour the Q1 UTB names — the WGB_NOT_OPPOSITE test. Tier (a) full credit needs the SAME behaviour turned around: UTB lack of sleep -> "get eight hours of sleep"; UTB lack of exercise -> "{{corpus:Q2/p6:response:30:57:sha=c00c372b3981}} week". `absent` when the goal is in the right territory but never says to do the behaviour more or less: "{{corpus:Q2/p10:response:31:56:sha=2bd894e2eb1a}}" against a UTB of {{corpus:Q3/p10:action:42:61:sha=00b9bd1557d3}} `absent` — it never says exercise more, and cost participant 10 two points; so is "{{corpus:Q2/p17:response:10:42:sha=efb4fb832473}} stay fit", which names a DIFFERENT MEASURE — pounds are not exercise. But the same behaviour's own positive STATE is `met`: "get back in shape" against a UTB of {{corpus:Q3/p10:action:42:61:sha=00b9bd1557d3:shape=C60000}} the inversion phrased as a state, and the graders charged participant 18 nothing for it. So a different measure of the right behaviour fails; the right behaviour's state passes. A goal naming a DIFFERENT ACTIVITY fails here too — "connect more with people and restart reading books" against a UTB of screen time never says to use the devices less, and cost participant 7 the item. Answer `absent` for that here even though the `wgb_is_counterpart` gate above may also catch it; do not pass this check assuming the gate will
- `reasons_listed` — `3`/`2`/`1`/`0`: how many statements the response OFFERS as reasons, counted off the page before judging any of them. This is not scored; it is the first half of the count below
- `reasons_failing` — `0`/`1`/`2`/`3`: of those, how many are NOT a benefit of the goal behaviour — either because the statement restates the harm of the unwanted behaviour rather than naming something the goal gets you, or because it repeats another statement already counted. Not scored; the second half of the count below
- `reasons_given` — `3`/`2`/`1`/`0`: HOW MANY separate BENEFITS of the goal behaviour — `reasons_listed` minus `reasons_failing`, the two counts above. Thematic overlap alone does not merge two benefits. Whether one sentence holds one benefit or two is STRUCTURAL, not a matter of degree. A second half that is a KNOCK-ON EFFECT of the first is ONE benefit — "{{corpus:Q2/p19:response:269:347:sha=7f0477c238f9:shape=C7be00000000000000}} learning" is one, not two, and counting it as two cost participant 19 a point. Two INDEPENDENT benefits merely joined by "and" are TWO — "{{corpus:Q2/p6:response:147:205:sha=ff93405cc948:shape=C380000000000}} also" is two, and counting it as one cost participant 6 a point. Three kinds of statement do not count at all. (i) A reason the UNWANTED behaviour is bad, rather than a benefit of the wanted one: against a WGB of "exercise more", "{{corpus:Q2/p3:response:45:78:sha=bb143acaf875}} lazy" and "it {{corpus:Q2/p3:response:93:120:sha=cbde2dc8bfda}}" are reasons to stop the UTB, and the graders counted them as ZERO benefits. (ii) A restatement of the goal or of the problem it solves: "{{corpus:Q2/p6:response:275:320:sha=447eb5403401}}" against a goal of being more active is the goal again, not a benefit of it; so is "{{corpus:Q2/p18:response:132:194:sha=5952b1969fc3}} shape" against a goal of getting back in shape. (iii) A statement that says the same thing as one already counted. Answer 3 for three or more that survive all three tests
- `uncertain` — `no`/`yes`: `yes` if any judgement above was a close call — this is rule 8's channel

DO NOT ANSWER `reason_1`, `reason_2`, `reason_3` individually. Answer `reasons_given` — HOW MANY you found — and the grader awards that many of them. Count them the way the guidance above says to, in one judgement over the whole response, rather than deciding each in isolation.

A GATE that is not satisfied is the whole story for this item: report that finding and do not dress it up with the checks underneath it.

## The behavior they chose (authoritative)
Chosen from the four on the list, before question 1.
<Ref id="bmod_h1_q2_llm_ref_utb" target="bmod_h1_utb" />

