which requirements are met, name any that are missing, and quote their own
words when you point something out. It must read as prose written to them: no
deduction codes, no point values, no score, and no check keys.

# Rubric item Q6 — 10 points

## Question asked of the student
Describe how you could change each of your two antecedents (from 4a) to get closer to your WGB. Then include how those changes may impact your consequences (from 4c).

## Credit components
- `state_a1` (1.25 pt): States the first antecedent being changed, and it matches 4a
- `change_a1` (1.25 pt): Describes HOW the first antecedent will be changed. Be generous about loose phrasing but strict about WHAT THE CHANGE ACTS ON: judge that, not how neatly it is put. Where the words after "by ..." describe the student performing their WGB, or improving some other habit, the antecedent itself is untouched and this is `not_described`. "{{corpus:Q6/p2:state_a2:0:74:sha=149afa8b3782}} {{corpus:Q6/p2:change_a2:0:35:sha=f1aa8f516c4c}}" leaves the video games exactly as they were — participant 2 lost 1.25 for it, the grader writing "{{corpus:Q6/p2:affect_c2:3:39:sha=7bc493d62ecd}} does not change your {{corpus:Q6/p2:state_a2:17:50:sha=7f56e7f06c95:shape=R33-0-22}}, and the 2.5/10 worked example is the same failure twice over. But the test is NOT whether the goal behaviour is mentioned — it is whether anything in the clause OPERATES ON the antecedent, and the goal behaviour is very often named as the change's expected RESULT. Participant 10's "{{corpus:Q6/p10:state_a1:0:80:sha=c89f409c6c63:shape=C1}} gym" counts: vague, but it acts on the laziness. So does participant 3's "{{corpus:Q6/p3:change_a1:0:70:sha=2aa4dbd3d492:shape=C1}}" against an antecedent of thinking exercise is unnecessary — the motivation acts on that belief and the gym is what follows from it; that response earned 10/10
- `state_a2` (1.25 pt): States the second antecedent being changed, and it matches 4a
- `change_a2` (1.25 pt): Describes HOW the second antecedent will be changed — the same test as `change_a1` above: judge what the change ACTS ON, and answer `not_described` when the clause only has the student doing their goal behaviour instead of touching the antecedent
- `state_c1` (1.25 pt): States the first consequence being affected, and it matches 4c
- `affect_c1` (1.25 pt): Describes HOW the first consequence will be affected
- `state_c2` (1.25 pt): States the second consequence being affected, and it matches 4c
- `affect_c2` (1.25 pt): Describes HOW the second consequence will be affected

## Deduction codes — the course's canonical wording for each gap
- `Q6_NONE` (-10): did not answer
- `A_NOT_STATED` (-1.25) [repeatable]: Did not state the antecedent from question 4a that is being changed.
- `A_MISMATCH` (-1.25) [repeatable]: This is a different antecedent from what you listed in question 4a. The antecedents must match up.
- `A_NO_CHANGE` (-1.25) [repeatable]: You do not describe how the antecedent(s) from question 4a will be changed.
- `C_MISMATCH` (-1.25) [repeatable]: This is a different consequence from what you listed in question 4c. The consequences must match up.
- `C_NO_EFFECT` (-1.25) [repeatable]: You do not describe how the consequence(s) will be affected by changing the antecedent(s).
- `C_NOT_STATED` (-1.25) [repeatable]: Did not state the consequence being affected.

## Grading guidance
- WORK THE EIGHT SLOTS IN ORDER. This item is a checklist, not an impression. Walk them one at a time and decide each independently: (1) is antecedent 1 stated, and does it match 4a? (2) is the change to antecedent 1 described? (3) is antecedent 2 stated, and does it match 4a? (4) is the change to antecedent 2 described? (5) is consequence 1 stated, and does it match 4c? (6) is the effect on consequence 1 described? (7) is consequence 2 stated, and does it match 4c? (8) is the effect on consequence 2 described?
- ANSWER ALL EIGHT, INDEPENDENTLY. You must return a verdict for every slot, even after you have found several failures. Do not stop early: a response can fail six of eight and still read fluently. Across this cohort the graders found 50 failed slots in 20 responses — an average of 2.5 per student — so a verdict sheet with only one or two failures is more likely to be an incomplete reading than a strong answer.
- PRESENCE IS NOT WORDING. Be generous about how loosely something is phrased and strict about whether it is there at all. "{{corpus:Q6/p10:state_a1:0:80:sha=c89f409c6c63}} gym" describes a change (participant 10, full credit) — loose but present. A paragraph that flows well while never naming the second consequence has an absent slot, however polished it reads.
- Slot verdicts: `met` when the element is present; `absent` when it is not there at all; `mismatch` when it IS there but is a different antecedent/consequence from the one in 4a/4c; `not_described` for a change/effect slot where the element is named but nothing is said about how it changes or is affected.
- Mismatch means a DIFFERENT item, not a reworded one. Participant 11 restated theirs in the template's own phrasing and earned 10/10. But participant 5's first antecedent was genuinely a different one from 4a and cost 1.25.
- A whole missing half — second antecedent and second consequence never addressed — is four absent slots and therefore 5.0, which is what participants 17 and 19 lost.
- Cross-item matching is still the most common deduction in the corpus (14 of 20 gold rows carry Q6 feedback), so check each stated antecedent against 4a and each stated consequence against 4c before crediting the slot.
- The grader's model answer template: "I can change my first antecedent of ___ by ___. This will impact my first consequence of ___ by ___. I can change my second antecedent of ___ by ___. This will impact my second consequence of ___ by ___."

## Worked examples
Three responses from other students in this cohort, with the verdict sheet the human grader's marks imply. Match your judgements to these. They are reference material only — never grade them.

### Full credit (10/10) — loose phrasing still counts
Their 4a: 1) One antecedent {{corpus:Q4a/p10:first:15:80:sha=4513d8e06599:shape=A4,A22}} 2) {{corpus:Q4a/p10:second:0:116:sha=ba8c8ba1d5ba:shape=A23,A86}}
Their 4c: 1) The consequences ... {{corpus:Q4c/p10:first:75:125:sha=d96f8eafcfee}} doing {{corpus:Q4c/p10:first:132:173:sha=954012fdd8ff}} 2) Another consequence ... {{corpus:Q4c/p10:second:78:118:sha=becf00cba25e}}
Their answer: 1) {{corpus:Q6/p10:state_a1:0:85:sha=30d070f3ee29}} {{corpus:Q6/p10:change_a1:0:92:sha=54719fc0ba56}} {{corpus:Q6/p10:affect_c1:0:78:sha=e78c71a63822:shape=A1}} 2) {{corpus:Q6/p10:state_a2:0:91:sha=96ddc929d970}} This will help reduce {{corpus:Q6/p10:change_a2:23:49:sha=865ea5a35836}} confidence. {{corpus:Q6/p10:affect_c2:0:83:sha=44225ba37dc3}}
Verdicts: state_a1=first, change_a1=met, state_a2=second, change_a2=met, state_c1=first, affect_c1=met, state_c2=second, affect_c2=met
Why: Every slot is loosely worded and every slot counts. "{{corpus:Q6/p10:state_a1:0:49:sha=e50c84f0c5e6}}" is a described change. "Good standing mentally" is the 4c mental-health consequence being affected. Do not demand the template's phrasing.

### 2.5/10 — antecedents named, but NO change to them described
Their 4a: My first antecedent {{corpus:Q4a/p8:first:19:58:sha=07eee26c9712}} gym. {{corpus:Q4a/p8:first:80:120:sha=ca075a14481c}} instead. My second trigger is {{corpus:Q4a/p8:second:26:60:sha=b6dda568cd34}} {{corpus:Q4a/p8:second:65:101:sha=2e6b1979b4e7}}
Their 4c: {{corpus:Q4c/p8:first:0:117:sha=9bdf68b7873f}} One consequence ... is that I have 0 motivation {{corpus:Q4c/p8:second:83:135:sha=d1b9021dfddc}}
Their answer: {{corpus:Q6/p8:state_a1:0:103:sha=c2229fd78f50}} Which then makes me wish I would have just gone to the gym. {{corpus:Q6/p8:change_a1:0:36:sha=3d9188d12f04}} Tuesday-Friday. {{corpus:Q6/p8:change_a1:54:108:sha=bc805c563129}} {{corpus:Q6/p8:state_a2:0:98:sha=ef418b549f10}} which can lead to {{corpus:Q1/p8:response:206:236:sha=a6fc82d76be7}} {{corpus:Q6/p8:change_a2:0:82:sha=39bfccf84972}} {{corpus:Q6/p8:change_a2:94:127:sha=815d1ebd00a1}} progress.
Verdicts: state_a1=second, change_a1=not_described, state_a2=first, change_a2=not_described, state_c1=absent, affect_c1=absent, state_c2=absent, affect_c2=absent
Why: THE MOST IMPORTANT CASE. Both antecedents are named, so both state slots are met. But "{{corpus:Q6/p8:change_a1:10:31:sha=59e2c4a3daa0}} Tuesday-Friday" and "{{corpus:Q6/p8:change_a2:9:52:sha=02595bb7f918}} week" describe DOING THE GOAL BEHAVIOUR, not changing the antecedent of laziness or of playing video games. A plan to perform the WGB is not a change to the antecedent, so both change slots are not_described. Neither 4c consequence is named at all, so all four consequence slots are absent. The grader wrote "did not say how each antecedent is being changed" and "did not state each consequence being affected".

### 6.25/10 — a mismatch plus a missing second consequence
Their 4a: {{corpus:Q4a/p6:first:0:63:sha=e72260775380}} {{corpus:Q4a/p6:second:0:56:sha=653e13080568}} days.
Their 4c: One consequence ... {{corpus:Q4c/p6:first:47:91:sha=0e6aa9251262}} distances. Another consequence ... {{corpus:Q4c/p6:second:51:122:sha=fc320e1e18ec}}
Their answer: I will be changing my antecedent of {{corpus:Q6/p6:state_a1:0:21:sha=641b355f6e09}} &amp; {{corpus:Q6/p6:state_a2:4:38:sha=56244251ed73}}, which results in muscle soreness and little to no flexibility. {{corpus:Q6/p6:change_a1:0:93:sha=2921663688ad}} while stretching daily. When I start to become more active within myself, {{corpus:Q6/p6:state_c1:0:91:sha=2600f49be406}} {{corpus:Q6/p6:affect_c1:0:57:sha=cffb42c551a1}}
Verdicts: state_a1=first, change_a1=met, state_a2=neither, change_a2=met, state_c1=second, affect_c1=met, state_c2=absent, affect_c2=absent
Why: The second antecedent addressed here is "{{corpus:Q6/p6:state_a1:0:21:sha=641b355f6e09}}", which is not 4a's "not being motivated" — that is a mismatch, not an absence. Only one 4c consequence (stiffness) is picked up; the second is never named, so both of its slots are absent.

## What the two `state_` checks report
These four do NOT report a match. They report an IDENTITY: which of the two listed items the box names. Answer `first` or `second` for 4a's first or second antecedent (4c's first or second consequence), `neither` if the box names something that is not one of them, and `absent` if it names no antecedent or consequence at all.

Say what you see and nothing more. Whether the pair covers both listed items is worked out from your two answers by the grader, so you do not need to reason about the other box, and a student who addresses 4a's second antecedent first has still addressed it — report `second` and it will be credited. The distinction between `neither` and `absent` is not about points, which are the same either way; it decides whether the student is told their antecedent does not match 4a or that they did not state one.

ONE THING TO CARRY INTO `feedback`. Each of the two listed items can be credited once, so if you report the SAME label for both boxes — `first` twice, say — the student has named one antecedent twice and left the other unaddressed, and only one of the two boxes can count. Your verdicts already handle the scoring; what you must not do is congratulate them on both boxes in that case. Say that the second repeats the first and name the item from 4a they have not yet addressed.

## The checklist to return (`checks`)
Return a verdict for EVERY one of these, in this order, BEFORE you write
`feedback` — a response can fail most of them and still read fluently. Each
check's verdicts are listed with the satisfied one first, unless a note above
says the check reports an identity rather than a judgement.

- `state_a1` — `first`/`second`/`neither`/`absent`: which of 4a's two antecedents does this box name? `first`, `second`, `neither` if it is not one of them, `absent` if none is named
- `change_a1` — `met`/`absent`/`mismatch`/`not_described`: Describes HOW the first antecedent will be changed. Be generous about loose phrasing but strict about WHAT THE CHANGE ACTS ON: judge that, not how neatly it is put. Where the words after "by ..." describe the student performing their WGB, or improving some other habit, the antecedent itself is untouched and this is `not_described`. "{{corpus:Q6/p2:state_a2:0:74:sha=149afa8b3782}} {{corpus:Q6/p2:change_a2:0:35:sha=f1aa8f516c4c}}" leaves the video games exactly as they were — participant 2 lost 1.25 for it, the grader writing "{{corpus:Q6/p2:affect_c2:3:39:sha=7bc493d62ecd}} does not change your {{corpus:Q6/p2:state_a2:17:50:sha=7f56e7f06c95:shape=R33-0-22}}, and the 2.5/10 worked example is the same failure twice over. But the test is NOT whether the goal behaviour is mentioned — it is whether anything in the clause OPERATES ON the antecedent, and the goal behaviour is very often named as the change's expected RESULT. Participant 10's "{{corpus:Q6/p10:state_a1:0:80:sha=c89f409c6c63:shape=C1}} gym" counts: vague, but it acts on the laziness. So does participant 3's "{{corpus:Q6/p3:change_a1:0:70:sha=2aa4dbd3d492:shape=C1}}" against an antecedent of thinking exercise is unnecessary — the motivation acts on that belief and the gym is what follows from it; that response earned 10/10
- `state_a2` — `first`/`second`/`neither`/`absent`: same question for the other box — `second` is the expected answer but `first` is credited too if that is what it names, because the grader pairs them
- `change_a2` — `met`/`absent`/`mismatch`/`not_described`: Describes HOW the second antecedent will be changed — the same test as `change_a1` above: judge what the change ACTS ON, and answer `not_described` when the clause only has the student doing their goal behaviour instead of touching the antecedent
- `state_c1` — `first`/`second`/`neither`/`absent`: which of 4c's two consequences does this box name? `first`, `second`, `neither` if it is not one of them, `absent` if none is named
- `affect_c1` — `met`/`absent`/`mismatch`/`not_described`: Describes HOW the first consequence will be affected
- `state_c2` — `first`/`second`/`neither`/`absent`: same question for the other box — either label is credited; the grader checks that between the two boxes both of 4c's consequences are named
- `affect_c2` — `met`/`absent`/`mismatch`/`not_described`: Describes HOW the second consequence will be affected
- `uncertain` — `no`/`yes`: `yes` if any judgement above was a close call — this is rule 8's channel

